WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.347
Many teams use a load balancer because clients want one endpoint.

2
00:00:04.347 --> 00:00:12.962
Chat UIs, coding assistants, automation scripts, and internal tools are easier to configure when they all call a single base URL.

3
00:00:12.962 --> 00:00:21.576
A reverse proxy, gateway, or load balancer can present that endpoint while distributing work across multiple local LLM servers.

4
00:00:21.576 --> 00:00:23.947
Compatibility depends on details.

5
00:00:23.947 --> 00:00:27.188
Endpoint paths must match client expectations.

6
00:00:27.188 --> 00:00:29.638
Model names may need stable aliases.

7
00:00:29.638 --> 00:00:34.459
Headers may carry authentication, tracing, or streaming preferences.

8
00:00:34.459 --> 00:00:39.596
Response fields and error codes should remain understandable to the client.

9
00:00:39.596 --> 00:00:47.025
A setup that works for one script may still fail for a coding assistant if it changes streaming or error behavior.

10
00:00:47.025 --> 00:00:50.503
Streaming compatibility is a common failure point.

11
00:00:50.503 --> 00:00:58.959
Some proxies buffer responses by default, turning an interactive token stream into a long pause followed by a complete answer.

12
00:00:58.959 --> 00:01:03.069
Others apply idle timeouts that interrupt slow generations.

13
00:01:03.069 --> 00:01:11.446
If a client expects server-sent events or chunked output, the entire path from client to backend must preserve that behavior.

14
00:01:11.446 --> 00:01:14.766
Authentication and testing belong in the design.

15
00:01:14.766 --> 00:01:21.800
A local endpoint may process source code, private documents, prompts, outputs, and accidental secrets.

16
00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:28.597
Centralizing access can simplify controls, but it also makes the gateway sensitive infrastructure.

17
00:01:28.597 --> 00:01:41.005
Before replacing direct backend connections, test the real chat UI, coding assistant, scripts, model selection, streaming, cancellation, retries, long prompts, and error handling.
